Good.

One possible issue: seed input max length 18 and `hashSeed` handles numeric strings but hashes them instead of using the number. The displayed seed is a 32-bit unsigned. Entering that number produces a different world (because hashSeed string). This is a UX inconsistency: the seed shown is a number, but typing the same number into the input will hash the string "3491790963" to a different seed. Let's verify `hashSeed`: if typeof value === 'number' return floor; if string, FNV hash. `newWorld` passes `seedText` string to URL, and `urlSeed` is a string, so `hashSeed(urlSeed)` hashes the string. Thus the title seed input, which shows the world seed number, if submitted, generates a different world. That's a bug/UX problem. We should fix it: in `hashSeed`, if the string is all digits and within safe range, parse it as a number and return it. Then entering the displayed seed reproduces the same world. But existing URL `?seed=799306442` currently produces hash 3491790963; after the fix, it would produce 799306442. That's fine, but our test expected seeds may change. More intuitive.

Let's modify `hashSeed`:
```js
function hashSeed(value) {
  if (typeof value === 'number' && Number.isFinite(value)) return Math.floor(value) >>> 0
  const text = String(value ?? '').trim()
  if (/^\d+$/.test(text)) {
    const n = Number(text)
    if (Number.isFinite(n) && n >= 0 && n <= 0xFFFFFFFF) return Math.floor(n) >>> 0
  }
  ...
}
```
This makes numeric seed input deterministic and matches the displayed seed. The URL seed test then expects seed 799306442, but we already changed the test to capture expected. It will still pass. The New World button random seed number will be passed as a string and parsed to the same number, so the displayed seed equals the random seed. Good.

This is worth fixing.

Also, if a user enters a negative number or decimal, it hashes; fine.

Another potential issue: `seed` is displayed as `world.seed`, which is 32-bit unsigned, but the input maxlength 18. Fine.

Let's patch hashSeed.

